{"source_id": "CP-S004", "chunk_id": "CP-S004-0001", "chunk_index": 0, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Data Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["data science", "skills", "wages", "outlook"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.718978+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Title: Data Scientists URL Source: https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm Markdown Content: * [Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-1) * [What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-2) * [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-3) * [How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-4) * [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-5) * [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-6) * [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-7) * [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-8) * [More Info](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-9) ## Summary Please enable javascript to play this video. [Video transcript available at https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uD8DfbJrqRI.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uD8DfbJrqRI) | Quick Facts: Data Scientists | | --- | | [2025 Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-wage) | $120,230 per year $57.80 per hour | | [Typical Entry-Level Education](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-education) | Bachelor's degree | | [Work Experience in a Related Occupation](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-experience) | None | | [On-the-job Training](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-training) | None | | [Number of Jobs, 2025](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-number-jobs) | 275,600 | | [Job Outlook, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-outlook) | 35% (Much faster than average) | | [Employment Change, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-emp-change) | 95,400 | ### [What Data Scientists Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-2) Data scientists use analytical tools and techniques to extract meaningful insights from data. ### [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-3) Data scientists spend much of their time in an office setting. Most work full time. ### [How to Become a Data Scientist](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-4) Data scientists typically need at least a bachelor’s degree in mathematics, statistics, computer science, or a related field to enter the occupation. Some employers require or prefer that applicants have a master’s or doctoral degree. ### [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-5) The median annual wage for data scientists was $120,230 in May 2025. ### [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-6) Employment of data scientists is projected to grow 35 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 24,800 openings for data scientists are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-7) Explore resources for employment and wages by state and area for data scientists. ### [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-8) Compare the job duties, education, job growth, and pay of data scientists with similar occupations. ### [More Information, Including Links to O*NET](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#tab-9) Learn more about data scientists by visiting additional resources, including O*NET, a source on key characteristics of workers and occupations. ## What Data Scientists Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 1: data-scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/96308.png) To present their findings, these scientists often make use of data visualization. Data scientists use analytical tools and techniques to extract meaningful insights from data. ### Duties Data scientists typically do the following: * Determine which data are available and useful for the project * Collect, categorize, and analyze data * Create, validate, test, and update algorithms and models * Use data visualization software to present findings * Make business recommendations to stakeholders based on data analysis Data scientists often begin a project by gathering or identifying relevant data sources, such as surveys. They"}
{"source_id": "CP-S004", "chunk_id": "CP-S004-0002", "chunk_index": 1, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Data Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["data science", "skills", "wages", "outlook"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.718978+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "to extract meaningful insights from data. ### Duties Data scientists typically do the following: * Determine which data are available and useful for the project * Collect, categorize, and analyze data * Create, validate, test, and update algorithms and models * Use data visualization software to present findings * Make business recommendations to stakeholders based on data analysis Data scientists often begin a project by gathering or identifying relevant data sources, such as surveys. They may use a variety of methods to obtain data, including through access to other organizations’ databases or by using web-scraping tools (software that extracts specific information from websites). They may start with large, unstructured datasets, commonly referred to as raw data. To properly analyze the data, these scientists must “clean” the raw data, a process by which they structure the data to make them readable by software programs. Data scientists develop algorithms (sets of instructions that tell computers what to do) and models to support programs for machine learning. They use machine learning to classify or categorize data or to make predictions related to the models. Scientists also must test the algorithms and models for accuracy, including for updates with newly collected data. Data scientists often use data visualization software to present their findings as charts, maps, and other graphics. Visualization techniques allow data scientists to clearly communicate their analyses to technical and nontechnical audiences, including colleagues, managers, and clients. Ensuring that audiences understand the information helps data scientists make recommendations for business decisions or process changes based on the results of their analysis. Some data scientists choose to focus on a particular area of work. For example, data scientists who have a strong coding or engineering background may develop or recommend systems, build machine learning algorithms, and devise ways to enhance web-browsing functions. Others conduct research for reports or academic journals. Still others focus on improving business strategy for activities such as marketing, sales, and user engagement. ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 2: data-scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/96310.png) Data scientists typically work in an office setting. Data scientists held about 275,600 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of data scientists were as follows: Publishing, broadcasting, and content providers 12% Insurance carriers and related activities 10 Computer systems design and related services 10 Management of companies and enterprises 10 Credit intermediation and related activities 6 Data scientists spend much of their time in an office setting. ### Work Schedules Most data scientists work full time. ## How to Become a Data Scientist[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image 3: data-scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/96309.png) Data scientists need strong computer skills. Data scientists typically need at least a bachelor’s degree in mathematics, statistics, computer science, or a related field to enter the occupation. However,"}
{"source_id": "CP-S004", "chunk_id": "CP-S004-0003", "chunk_index": 2, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Data Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["data science", "skills", "wages", "outlook"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.718978+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "services 10 Management of companies and enterprises 10 Credit intermediation and related activities 6 Data scientists spend much of their time in an office setting. ### Work Schedules Most data scientists work full time. ## How to Become a Data Scientist[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image 3: data-scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/96309.png) Data scientists need strong computer skills. Data scientists typically need at least a bachelor’s degree in mathematics, statistics, computer science, or a related field to enter the occupation. However, some employers require or prefer that candidates have a master’s or doctoral degree. ### Education Data scientists typically need at least a bachelor’s degree, but some jobs require a master’s or doctoral degree. Common [fields of degree](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/home.htm) include [mathematics](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/mathematics/mathematics-field-of-degree.htm), statistics, [computer science](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/computer-and-information/computer-and-information-technology-field-of-degree.htm), [business](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/business/business-field-of-degree.htm), and [engineering](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/engineering/engineering-field-of-degree.htm). Because data science involves the use of algorithms and statistical techniques, students need extensive study in mathematics and statistics. High school students interested in becoming data scientists should take classes in subjects such as linear algebra, calculus, and probability and statistics. At the college level, courses in computer science are important in addition to math and statistics. Students must learn data-oriented programming languages as well as statistical, database, and other software for presenting analyses. ### Other Experience Some employers require industry-related experience or education. For example, data scientists seeking work in an asset management company may need to have experience in the finance industry or to have completed coursework that demonstrates an understanding of investments, banking, or related subjects. ### Important Qualities The following are examples of qualities that are important for these workers to perform their duties. For more information, visit the Employment Projections (EP) [skills data page](https://www.bls.gov/emp/data/skills-data.htm). **_Analytical skills._**Data scientists must be adept at researching and at examining and interpreting findings. **_Computer skills._** Data scientists must be able to write code, analyze data, develop or improve algorithms, and use data visualization tools. **_Communication skills._** Data scientists must be able to convey the results of their analysis to technical and nontechnical audiences to make business recommendations. _**Logical-thinking skills.**_ Data scientists must understand and be able to design and develop statistical models and to analyze data. **_Math skills._** Data scientists use statistical methods to collect and organize data. **_Problem-solving skills._**Data scientists must devise solutions to the problems they encounter in data collection and cleaning and in developing statistical models and algorithms. ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Data Scientists Median annual wages, May 2025 Data scientists $120,230 Mathematical science occupations $107,570 Total, all occupations $50,980 The median annual wage for data scientists was $120,230 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $67,240, and the highest"}
{"source_id": "CP-S004", "chunk_id": "CP-S004-0004", "chunk_index": 3, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Data Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["data science", "skills", "wages", "outlook"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.718978+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "in developing statistical models and algorithms. ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Data Scientists Median annual wages, May 2025 Data scientists $120,230 Mathematical science occupations $107,570 Total, all occupations $50,980 The median annual wage for data scientists was $120,230 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $67,240, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $199,130. In May 2025, the median annual wages for data scientists in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Publishing, broadcasting, and content providers$142,240 Computer systems design and related services 132,380 Credit intermediation and related activities 129,490 Management of companies and enterprises 128,050 Insurance carriers and related activities 108,650 Most data scientists work full time. ## Job Outlook[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-job-outlook) ### Data Scientists Percent change in employment, projected 2025–35 Data scientists 35% Mathematical science occupations 25% Total, all occupations 3% Employment of data scientists is projected to grow 35 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 24,800 openings for data scientists are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### Employment Employment growth for data scientists is expected to stem from an increased demand for data-driven decisions. The volume of data available and the potential uses for that data will increase over the projections decade. As a result, organizations will likely need more data scientists to analyze the large amounts of information and data collected. Moreover, firms are expected to continue to integrate artificial intelligence (AI)-based systems into their workflows. Data scientists will be needed to help businesses leverage AI and other technologies to enhance their business processes, to make informed decisions, to design and develop new products, and to better market their products. Employment projections data for data scientists, 2025–35 | Occupational Title | SOC Code | Employment, 2025 | Projected Employment, 2035 | Change, 2025–35 | Employment by Industry | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values are rounded to the nearest 100 or percent. Data is unavailable for values denoted with a \"—\". Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. | | **Data scientists** | 15-2051 | 275,600 | 371,000 | 35 | 95,400 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-2051&ioType=o) | ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ###"}
{"source_id": "CP-S004", "chunk_id": "CP-S004-0005", "chunk_index": 4, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Data Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["data science", "skills", "wages", "outlook"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.718978+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "| | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values are rounded to the nearest 100 or percent. Data is unavailable for values denoted with a \"—\". Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. | | **Data scientists** | 15-2051 | 275,600 | 371,000 | 35 | 95,400 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-2051&ioType=o) | ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ### **Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)** The [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics](https://www.bls.gov/oes/) (OEWS) program produces employment and wage estimates annually for over 800 occupations. These estimates are available for the nation as a whole, for individual states, and for metropolitan and nonmetropolitan areas. The link below goes to OEWS data maps for employment and wages by state and area. Use the dropdown boxes to select an occupation. * [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) Profiles](https://data.bls.gov/oesprofile/) ### **Projections Central** Occupational employment projections are developed for all states by Labor Market Information (LMI) or individual state Employment Projections offices. All state projections data are available at [https://projectionscentral.org](https://projectionscentral.org/). Information on this site allows projected employment growth for an occupation to be compared among states or to be compared within one state. In addition, states may produce projections for areas; there are links to each state’s websites where these data may be retrieved. ### **CareerOneStop** CareerOneStop includes hundreds of [occupational profiles](http://www.careerinfonet.org/Occupations/select_occupation.aspx?next=occ_rep&level=&optstatus=111111111&id=1&nodeid=2&soccode=&stfips=&jobfam=&menuMode) with data available by state and metro area. There are links in the left-hand side menu to compare occupational employment by state and occupational wages by local area or metro area. There is also a [salary info tool](https://www.careeronestop.org/Toolkit/Wages/find-salary.aspx?frd=true) to search for wages by zip code. ## Similar Occupations[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-similar-jobs) This table shows a list of occupations with job duties that are similar to those of data scientists. | | Occupation | Job Duties | ENTRY-LEVEL EDUCATION [![Image 4: Help on Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_help.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=inlinehelp-education) | 2025 MEDIAN PAY [![Image 5: Help on Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_help.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=inlinehelp-pay) | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | [![Image 6: Actuaries](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1828.jpg)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/actuaries.htm) | **[Actuaries](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/actuaries.htm)** | Actuaries use mathematics, statistics, and financial theory to analyze the economic costs of risk and uncertainty. | Bachelor's degree | $130,000 | | [![Image 7: Computer and information research scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1504.jpg)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm) | **[Computer and Information Research Scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm)** | Computer and information research scientists design innovative uses for new and existing computing technology. | Master's degree | $140,300 | | [![Image 8: Economists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1522.jpg)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/economists.htm) | **[Economists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/economists.htm)** | Economists conduct research, prepare reports, and evaluate issues related to monetary and fiscal policy. They also may collect and analyze statistical data. | Master's degree | $124,720 | | [![Image 9: Financial analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1757.jpg)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/financial-analysts.htm) | **[Financial Analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/business-and-financial/financial-analysts.htm)** | Financial analysts research and evaluate"}
