mmary Please enable javascript to play this video. ![Image 8: YouTube Video](https://i.ytimg.com/vi/T0JdNufvn-o/hqdefault.jpg) [Video transcript available at https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T0JdNufvn-o.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T0JdNufvn-o) | Quick Facts: Computer Systems Analysts | | --- | | [2025 Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-wage) | $105,850 per year $50.89 per hour | | [Typical Entry-Level Education](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-education) | Bachelor's degree | | [Work Experience in a Related Occupation](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-experience) | None | | [On-the-job Training](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-training) | None | | [Number of Jobs, 2025](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-number-jobs) | 544,400 | | [Job Outlook, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-outlook) | 8% (Much faster than average) | | [Employment Change, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-emp-change) | 42,900 | ### [What Computer Systems Analysts Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-2) Computer systems analysts study an organization’s current computer systems and design ways to improve efficiency. ### [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-3) Most computer systems analysts work full time. ### [How to Become a Computer Systems Analyst](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-4) Computer systems analysts typically need a bachelor’s degree to enter the occupation. Studying a computer science or information systems field is common, although not always a requirement. Some firms hire job candidates who have a degree in business or liberal arts along with relevant skills. ### [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-5) The median annual wage for computer systems analysts was $105,850 in May 2025. ### [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-6) Employment of computer systems analysts is projected to grow 8 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 32,900 openings for computer systems analysts are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-7) Explore resources for employment and wages by state and area for computer systems analysts. ### [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-8) Compare the job duties, education, job growth, and pay of computer systems analysts with similar occupations. ### [More Information, Including Links to O*NET](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-9) Learn more about computer systems analysts by visiting additional resources, including O*NET, a source on key characteristics of workers and occupations. [What They Do ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-2) ## What Computer Systems Analysts Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 9: Computer systems analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1805.jpg) Analysts create diagrams to help programmers and architects build computer systems. Computer systems analysts, sometimes called _systems architects_, study an organization’s current computer systems and procedures and design improvements to them. In doing so, these analysts help the organization operate more efficiently. ### Duties Computer systems analysts typically do the following: * Consult with managers to determine the role of information technology (IT) systems"}
{"source_id": "CP-S007", "chunk_id": "CP-S007-0004", "chunk_index": 3, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer Systems Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["systems", "analysis", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719572+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Computer Systems Analysts Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 9: Computer systems analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1805.jpg) Analysts create diagrams to help programmers and architects build computer systems. Computer systems analysts, sometimes called _systems architects_, study an organization’s current computer systems and procedures and design improvements to them. In doing so, these analysts help the organization operate more efficiently. ### Duties Computer systems analysts typically do the following: * Consult with managers to determine the role of information technology (IT) systems in an organization * Research different technologies to decide if they would increase the organization’s efficiency * Analyze costs and benefits of IT systems and upgrades to help managers decide which, if any, to install * Devise ways to add functionality to existing computer systems * Design new systems by configuring hardware and software * Oversee the installation and configuration of new systems and customize them for the organization * Test systems to ensure that they work as expected * Write instruction manuals and train the systems’ end users Most computer systems analysts specialize in computer systems that are specific to their organization type. For example, an analyst might work with financial computer systems or with engineering computer systems. Computer systems analysts work with other IT team members to help an organization’s business leaders understand how computer systems best serve the organization. Computer systems analysts use a variety of techniques, such as data modeling, to design computer systems. Data modeling allows analysts to view processes and data flows. Analysts conduct indepth tests and analyze information and trends in the data to increase a system’s efficiency. Analysts calculate requirements for how much memory, storage, and computing power the computer system needs. They prepare diagrams for programmers or engineers to use when building the system. Analysts also work with these people to solve problems that arise after the initial system setup. Most analysts do some programming in the course of their work. Analysts who focus on coding and debugging, in addition to their other tasks, may be referred to as _programmer analysts._ They also may design and update their system’s software and create applications tailored to their organization’s needs. For information about other occupations that do programming or testing, see the profiles on [computer programmers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-programmers.htm) and [software developers, quality assurance analysts, and testers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/software-developers.htm). In some cases, analysts who supervise the installation or upgrade of IT systems from start to finish may be called _IT project managers_. They monitor a project’s progress to ensure that deadlines, standards, and cost targets are met. IT project managers who also plan and direct an organization’s IT department or IT policies are included in the profile on [computer and information systems managers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/computer-and-information-systems-managers.htm \"Computer and Information Systems Managers\"). [<- Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-1)[Work Environment ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-3) ##"}
{"source_id": "CP-S007", "chunk_id": "CP-S007-0005", "chunk_index": 4, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer Systems Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["systems", "analysis", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719572+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "testers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/software-developers.htm). In some cases, analysts who supervise the installation or upgrade of IT systems from start to finish may be called _IT project managers_. They monitor a project’s progress to ensure that deadlines, standards, and cost targets are met. IT project managers who also plan and direct an organization’s IT department or IT policies are included in the profile on [computer and information systems managers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/computer-and-information-systems-managers.htm \"Computer and Information Systems Managers\"). [<- Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-1)[Work Environment ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-3) ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 10: Computer systems analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1807.jpg) Some systems analysts work as consultants. Computer systems analysts held about 544,400 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of computer systems analysts were as follows: Computer systems design and related services 23% Finance and insurance 13 Management of companies and enterprises 10 Government, excluding state and local education and hospitals 7 Information 7 Computer systems analysts may work directly for an organization or as contractors, often for an information technology firm. The projects that computer systems analysts work on usually require them to collaborate with others. ### Work Schedules Most computer systems analysts work full time. [<- What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-2)[How to Become One ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-4) ## How to Become a Computer Systems Analyst[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image 11: Computer systems analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/3639.jpg) Most computer systems analysts have a bachelor’s degree. Computer systems analysts typically need a bachelor’s degree to enter the occupation. Studying a computer science or information systems field is common, although not always a requirement. Some firms hire job candidates who have a degree in business or liberal arts along with relevant skills. ### Education Computer systems analysts typically need a bachelor's [degree](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/home.htm)in [computer and information technology](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/computer-and-information/computer-and-information-technology-field-of-degree.htm) or a related field, such as [mathematics](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/mathematics/mathematics-field-of-degree.htm). Because these analysts are involved in the business side of an organization, taking business courses or majoring in management information systems may be helpful. Some employers hire job candidates who have liberal arts degrees and have gained programming or technical expertise elsewhere. Some employers prefer applicants who have a master’s degree in business administration (MBA) with a concentration in information systems. For technically complex jobs, a master’s degree in computer science may be more appropriate. Systems analysts may take continuing education courses throughout their careers to stay abreast of new technology. Technological advances are common in the computer field, and continual study is necessary to remain competitive. Systems analysts also must understand the industry they are working in. For example, an analyst working in a hospital may need a thorough understanding of healthcare plans and programs such as Medicare and Medicaid, and an analyst working for a bank may need to understand finance. Having industry-specific knowledge helps systems analysts communicate with managers to determine the role of the information technology (IT)"}
{"source_id": "CP-S007", "chunk_id": "CP-S007-0006", "chunk_index": 5, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer Systems Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["systems", "analysis", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719572+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "the computer field, and continual study is necessary to remain competitive. Systems analysts also must understand the industry they are working in. For example, an analyst working in a hospital may need a thorough understanding of healthcare plans and programs such as Medicare and Medicaid, and an analyst working for a bank may need to understand finance. Having industry-specific knowledge helps systems analysts communicate with managers to determine the role of the information technology (IT) systems in an organization. ### Advancement With experience, systems analysts may advance to become project managers and lead a team of analysts. Some eventually become IT directors or chief technology officers. For more information, see the profile on [computer and information systems managers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/computer-and-information-systems-managers.htm \"Computer and Information Systems Managers\"). ### Important Qualities The following are examples of qualities that are important for these workers to perform their duties. For more information, visit the Employment Projections (EP) [skills data page](https://www.bls.gov/emp/data/skills-data.htm). **_Analytical skills_.** Analysts must interpret complex information from various sources and decide the best way to move forward on a project. They must also figure out how changes may affect the project. **_Business skills._** Analysts design and implement computer systems or upgrade existing systems to meet an organization’s business goals. Analysts must have a thorough understanding of their organization’s business objectives in order to meet its needs. **_Communication skills_.** Analysts work as a liaison between management and the IT department and must explain complex issues in a way that both understand. **_Creativity._** Because analysts are tasked with finding innovative solutions to computer problems, they must be resourceful and use ingenuity in their work. **_Detail oriented._** Analysts study an organization’s computer systems and must pay attention to the minutiae to find areas of inefficiency or error. **_Organizational skills._** Analysts may coordinate work with different areas of an organization and must keep track of many tasks and deadlines to ensure that projects proceed according to plan. [<- Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-3)[Pay ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-5) ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Computer Systems Analysts Median annual wages, May 2025 Computer occupations $109,470 Computer systems analysts $105,850 Total, all occupations $50,980 Note: Data are from the Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics program. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. The median annual wage for computer systems analysts was $105,850 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $67,340, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $167,710. In May 2025, the median annual wages for computer systems analysts in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Computer systems"}
{"source_id": "CP-S007", "chunk_id": "CP-S007-0007", "chunk_index": 6, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer Systems Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["systems", "analysis", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719572+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "for computer systems analysts was $105,850 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $67,340, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $167,710. In May 2025, the median annual wages for computer systems analysts in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Computer systems design and related services$108,270 Management of companies and enterprises 107,980 Information 107,240 Finance and insurance 106,000 Government, excluding state and local education and hospitals 100,190 Most computer systems analysts work full time. [<- How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-4)[Job Outlook ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-6) ## Job Outlook[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-job-outlook) ### Computer Systems Analysts Percent change in employment, projected 2025–35 Computer systems analysts 8% Computer occupations 6% Total, all occupations 3% Note: Percent change in employment is rounded to the nearest whole number. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Employment of computer systems analysts is projected to grow 8 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 32,900 openings for computer systems analysts are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### Employment As organizations across the economy continue to rely on and expand information technology (IT), including artificial intelligence (AI), computer systems analysts will be hired to design and install new computer systems. Small firms with minimal IT requirements may find it more cost effective to contract with outside firms for these services rather than to hire computer systems analysts directly. Employment projections data for computer systems analysts, 2025–35 | Occupational Title | SOC Code | Employment, 2025 | Projected Employment, 2035 | Change, 2025–35 | Employment by Industry | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values are rounded to the nearest 100 or percent. Data is unavailable for values denoted with a \"—\". Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. | | **Computer systems analysts** | 15-1211 | 544,400 | 587,200 | 8 | 42,900 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-1211&ioType=o) | [<- Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-5)[State & Area Data ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#tab-7) ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-systems-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ### **Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics 