{"source_id": "CP-S008", "chunk_id": "CP-S008-0003", "chunk_index": 2, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer and Information Research Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["research", "AI", "computer science"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719750+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Outlook Handbook](https://www.bls.gov/images/ooh/ooh_title_image.png)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/) [Occupational Outlook Handbook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/home.htm)>[Computer and Information Technology](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/home.htm)> # Computer and Information Research Scientists [PRINTER-FRIENDLY ![Image 7: Print](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_printer.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#) * [Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-1) * [What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-2) * [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-3) * [How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-4) * [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-5) * [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-6) * [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-7) * [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-8) * [More Info](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-9) ## Summary Please enable javascript to play this video. ![Image 8: YouTube Video](https://i.ytimg.com/vi/jlZucw7_qWU/hqdefault.jpg) [Video transcript available at https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlZucw7_qWU.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jlZucw7_qWU) | Quick Facts: Computer and Information Research Scientists | | --- | | [2025 Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-wage) | $140,300 per year $67.45 per hour | | [Typical Entry-Level Education](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-education) | Master's degree | | [Work Experience in a Related Occupation](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-experience) | None | | [On-the-job Training](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-training) | None | | [Number of Jobs, 2025](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-number-jobs) | 38,600 | | [Job Outlook, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-outlook) | 22% (Much faster than average) | | [Employment Change, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-emp-change) | 8,400 | ### [What Computer and Information Research Scientists Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-2) Computer and information research scientists design innovative uses for new and existing computing technology. ### [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-3) Most computer and information research scientists work full time. ### [How to Become a Computer and Information Research Scientist](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-4) Computer and information research scientists typically need at least a master’s degree in computer science or a related field. In the federal government, a bachelor’s degree may be sufficient for some jobs. ### [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-5) The median annual wage for computer and information research scientists was $140,300 in May 2025. ### [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-6) Employment of computer and information research scientists is projected to grow 22 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 2,900 openings for computer and information research scientists are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-7) Explore resources for employment and wages by state and area for computer and information research scientists. ### [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-8) Compare the job duties, education, job growth, and pay of computer and information research scientists with similar occupations. ### [More Information, Including Links to O*NET](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-9) Learn more about computer and information research scientists by visiting additional resources, including O*NET, a source on key characteristics of workers and occupations. [What They Do ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-2) ## What Computer and Information Research Scientists Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 9: Computer and information research scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1502.jpg) Some computer scientists create programs to control robots. Computer and information research scientists design innovative uses for new and existing technology. They study and solve complex problems in computing for business, science, medicine, and other fields. ### Duties Computer and information research scientists typically do the following: * Explore problems"}
{"source_id": "CP-S008", "chunk_id": "CP-S008-0004", "chunk_index": 3, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer and Information Research Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["research", "AI", "computer science"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719750+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "of workers and occupations. [What They Do ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-2) ## What Computer and Information Research Scientists Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 9: Computer and information research scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1502.jpg) Some computer scientists create programs to control robots. Computer and information research scientists design innovative uses for new and existing technology. They study and solve complex problems in computing for business, science, medicine, and other fields. ### Duties Computer and information research scientists typically do the following: * Explore problems in computing and develop theories and models to address those problems * Collaborate with scientists and engineers to solve complex computing problems * Determine computing needs and system requirements * Develop new computing languages, software systems, and other tools to improve how people work with computers * Design and conduct experiments to test the operation of software systems, frequently using techniques from data science and machine learning * Analyze the results of their experiments * Write papers for publication and present research findings at conferences Computer and information research scientists create and improve computer software and hardware. To create and improve software, computer and information research scientists work with algorithms: sets of instructions that tell a computer what to do. Some difficult computing tasks require complex algorithms, which these scientists simplify to make computer systems as efficient as possible. These simplified algorithms may lead to advancements in many types of technology, such as machine learning systems and cloud computing. To improve computer hardware, these scientists design computer architecture. Their work may result in increased efficiencies, such as better networking technology, faster computing speeds, and improved information security. The following are examples of specialties for computer and information research scientists: **_Programming._** Some computer and information research scientists study and design new programming languages that are used to write software. New languages make software writing efficient by improving an existing language, such as Java, or by simplifying a specific aspect of programming, such as image processing. **_Robotics_.** These scientists study the development and application of robots. They explore how a machine can interact with the physical world. For example, they may create systems that control the robots or design robots to have features such as information processing or sensory feedback. Some computer and information research scientists work on multidisciplinary projects with [electrical engineers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/electrical-and-electronics-engineers.htm), [computer hardware engineers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/computer-hardware-engineers.htm),and other specialists. For example, robotics specialists and engineers who design robots’ hardware may team up to test whether the robots complete tasks as intended. [<- Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-1)[Work Environment ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-3) ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 10: Computer and information research scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1500.jpg) Computer and information research scientists improve ways to sort, manage, and display data. Computer and information research scientists held about 38,600 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of computer"}
{"source_id": "CP-S008", "chunk_id": "CP-S008-0005", "chunk_index": 4, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer and Information Research Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["research", "AI", "computer science"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719750+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "engineers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/electrical-and-electronics-engineers.htm), [computer hardware engineers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/architecture-and-engineering/computer-hardware-engineers.htm),and other specialists. For example, robotics specialists and engineers who design robots’ hardware may team up to test whether the robots complete tasks as intended. [<- Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-1)[Work Environment ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-3) ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 10: Computer and information research scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1500.jpg) Computer and information research scientists improve ways to sort, manage, and display data. Computer and information research scientists held about 38,600 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of computer and information research scientists were as follows: Federal government, excluding postal service 31% Computer systems design and related services 16 Research and development in the physical, engineering, and life sciences 14 Colleges, universities, and professional schools; state 7 Software publishers 3 Some scientists collaborate with engineers or other specialists or research scientists in different locations and do much of their work online. ### Work Schedules Most computer and information research scientists work full time. [<- What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-2)[How to Become One ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-4) ## How to Become a Computer and Information Research Scientist[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image 11: computer and information research scientists image](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/15191.jpg) Some computer scientists specialize in computer languages. Computer and information research scientists typically need at least a master’s degree in computer science or a related field. In the federal government, a bachelor’s degree may be sufficient for some jobs. ### Education Computer and information research scientists typically need a master’s or higher degree in computer science or a related field, such as computer engineering. A master’s degree usually requires 2 to 3 years of study after earning a bachelor’s degree in a computer-related field, such as computer science or information systems. Some employers prefer to hire candidates who have a Ph.D. Others, such as the federal government, may hire candidates who have a bachelor’s [degree](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/home.htm) in [computer and information technology](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/computer-and-information/computer-and-information-technology-field-of-degree.htm). Computer and information research scientists who work in a specialized field may need knowledge of that field. For example, those working on biomedical applications may need to have studied biology. ### Advancement Some computer and information research scientists advance to become [computer and information systems managers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/computer-and-information-systems-managers.htm). ### Important Qualities The following are examples of qualities that are important for these workers to perform their duties. For more information, visit the Employment Projections (EP) [skills data page](https://www.bls.gov/emp/data/skills-data.htm). **_Analytical skills._** Computer and information research scientists must be organized in their thinking to evaluate the results of their research. **_Communication skills._** Computer and information research scientists must be able to clearly explain their research, including to a nontechnical audience. They write papers for publication and present their research at conferences. **_Detail oriented._** Computer and information research scientists must pay close attention to their work, such as when testing the systems they design. Small programming errors could"}
{"source_id": "CP-S008", "chunk_id": "CP-S008-0006", "chunk_index": 5, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer and Information Research Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["research", "AI", "computer science"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719750+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Computer and information research scientists must be organized in their thinking to evaluate the results of their research. **_Communication skills._** Computer and information research scientists must be able to clearly explain their research, including to a nontechnical audience. They write papers for publication and present their research at conferences. **_Detail oriented._** Computer and information research scientists must pay close attention to their work, such as when testing the systems they design. Small programming errors could affect an entire project. **_Interpersonal skills._** Computer and information research scientists must work effectively with programmers and managers. They also may be on teams with engineers or other specialists. **_Logical thinking._**Computer and information research scientists must use sound reasoning when working on algorithms. **_Math skills._** Computer and information research scientists need a solid grasp of advanced math and other technical subjects critical to computing. **_Problem-solving skills._** Computer and information research scientists must think creatively to find innovative solutions in their research. [<- Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-3)[Pay ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-5) ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Computer and Information Research Scientists Median annual wages, May 2025 Computer and information research scientists $140,300 Computer occupations $109,470 Total, all occupations $50,980 Note: Data are from the Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics program. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. The median annual wage for computer and information research scientists was $140,300 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $82,200, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $230,630. In May 2025, the median annual wages for computer and information research scientists in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Software publishers$211,270 Computer systems design and related services 162,760 Research and development in the physical, engineering, and life sciences 141,830 Federal government, excluding postal service 124,870 Colleges, universities, and professional schools; state 85,460 Most computer and information research scientists work full time. [<- How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-4)[Job Outlook ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-6) ## Job Outlook[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-job-outlook) ### Computer and Information Research Scientists Percent change in employment, projected 2025–35 Computer and information research scientists 22% Computer occupations 6% Total, all occupations 3% Note: Percent change in employment is rounded to the nearest whole number. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Employment of computer and information research scientists is projected to grow 22 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 2,900 openings for computer and information research scientists are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected"}
{"source_id": "CP-S008", "chunk_id": "CP-S008-0007", "chunk_index": 6, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Computer and Information Research Scientists", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["research", "AI", "computer science"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:41.719750+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Percent change in employment is rounded to the nearest whole number. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Employment of computer and information research scientists is projected to grow 22 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 2,900 openings for computer and information research scientists are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### Employment The research and development conducted by computer and information research scientists turn ideas into technology. As demand for new and better technology grows, demand for computer and information research scientists is projected to grow as well. For example, computer scientists’ expertise will be needed in the creation of new technologies related to artificial intelligence (AI). Rapid growth in data collection by businesses is projected to lead to an increased need for data-mining services. Computer and information research scientists may develop new technology to help businesses make sense of very large amounts of data. A growing emphasis on cybersecurity also is projected to lead to new jobs because computer and information research scientists may study innovative ways to prevent potential cyberattacks. Employment projections data for computer and information research scientists, 2025–35 | Occupational Title | SOC Code | Employment, 2025 | Projected Employment, 2035 | Change, 2025–35 | Employment by Industry | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values are rounded to the nearest 100 or percent. Data is unavailable for values denoted with a \"—\". Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. | | **Computer and information research scientists** | 15-1221 | 38,600 | 47,000 | 22 | 8,400 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-1221&ioType=o) | [<- Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-5)[State & Area Data ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#tab-7) ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/computer-and-information-research-scientists.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ### **Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)** The [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics](https://www.bls.gov/oes/) (OEWS) program produces employment and wage estimates annually for over 800 occupations. These estimates are available for the nation as a whole, for individual states, and for metropolitan and nonmetropolitan areas. The link below goes to OEWS data maps for employment and wages by state and area. Use the dropdown boxes to select an occupation. * [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) Profiles](https://data.bls.gov/oesprofile/) ### **Projections Central** Occupational employment projections are developed for all states by Labor Market Information (LMI) or individual state Employment Projections offices. All state projections data are available at"}
