{"source_id": "CP-S009", "chunk_id": "CP-S009-0003", "chunk_index": 2, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Database Administrators and Architects", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["database", "cloud", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.490145+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Handbook](https://www.bls.gov/images/ooh/ooh_title_image.png)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/) [Occupational Outlook Handbook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/home.htm)>[Computer and Information Technology](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/home.htm)> # Database Administrators and Architects [PRINTER-FRIENDLY ![Image 7: Print](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_printer.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#) * [Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-1) * [What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-2) * [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-3) * [How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-4) * [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-5) * [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-6) * [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-7) * [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-8) * [More Info](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-9) ## Summary Please enable javascript to play this video. ![Image 8: YouTube Video](https://i.ytimg.com/vi/GKAnWFOyk8E/hqdefault.jpg) [Video transcript available at https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GKAnWFOyk8E.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GKAnWFOyk8E) | Quick Facts: Database Administrators and Architects | | --- | | [2025 Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-wage) | $126,760 per year $60.94 per hour | | [Typical Entry-Level Education](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-education) | Bachelor's degree | | [Work Experience in a Related Occupation](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-experience) | [See How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-4) | | [On-the-job Training](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-training) | None | | [Number of Jobs, 2025](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-number-jobs) | 144,500 | | [Job Outlook, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-outlook) | 4% (As fast as average) | | [Employment Change, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-emp-change) | 6,500 | ### [What Database Administrators and Architects Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-2) Database administrators and architects create or organize systems to store and secure data. ### [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-3) Many database administrators and architects work in firms that provide computer design services or in industries that have large databases, such educational institutions and insurance companies. Most database administrators and architects work full time. ### [How to Become a Database Administrator or Architect](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-4) Database administrators and architects typically need a bachelor’s degree in computer and information technology or a related field. ### [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-5) The median annual wage for database administrators was $104,620 in May 2025. The median annual wage for database architects was $139,500 in May 2025. ### [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-6) Overall employment of database administrators and architects is projected to grow 4 percent from 2025 to 2035, about as fast as the average for all occupations. About 7,300 openings for database administrators and architects are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-7) Explore resources for employment and wages by state and area for database administrators and architects. ### [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-8) Compare the job duties, education, job growth, and pay of database administrators and architects with similar occupations. ### [More Information, Including Links to O*NET](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-9) Learn more about database administrators and architects by visiting additional resources, including O*NET, a source on key characteristics of workers and occupations. [What They Do ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-2) ## What Database Administrators and Architects Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 9: Database administrators](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1810.jpg) Database administrators ensure databases run efficiently. Database administrators and architects create or organize systems to store and secure a variety of data, such as financial information and customer shipping records. They also make sure that the data are available"}
{"source_id": "CP-S009", "chunk_id": "CP-S009-0004", "chunk_index": 3, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Database Administrators and Architects", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["database", "cloud", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.490145+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "database administrators and architects by visiting additional resources, including O*NET, a source on key characteristics of workers and occupations. [What They Do ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-2) ## What Database Administrators and Architects Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 9: Database administrators](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1810.jpg) Database administrators ensure databases run efficiently. Database administrators and architects create or organize systems to store and secure a variety of data, such as financial information and customer shipping records. They also make sure that the data are available to authorized users. ### Duties Database administrators and architects typically do the following: * Identify user needs to create and administer databases * Design and build new databases * Ensure that organizational data are secure * Back up and restore data to prevent data loss * Ensure that databases operate efficiently and without error * Make and test modifications to database structure when needed * Maintain databases and update permissions Database administrators, often called DBAs, make sure that data analysts and other users can easily use databases to find the information they need. They also ensure that systems perform as they should by monitoring database operation and providing support. Many databases contain personal, proprietary, or financial information. Database administrators often are responsible for planning security measures to protect this important information. Database architects design and build new databases for systems and applications. They research the technical requirements of an organization during the design phase and then create models for building the database. Finally, they code new data architecture, integrating existing databases or infrastructure, and check for errors or inefficiencies. The duties of database administrators and database architects may overlap. For example, administrators and architects may be generalists who work on both systems and applications. However, some DBAs specialize in certain tasks, such as maintenance, that vary with an organization and its needs. Two common specialties are as follows: **_System DBAs_** are responsible for the physical and technical aspects of a database, such as installing upgrades and patches to fix program bugs. They ensure that the firm’s database management systems work properly. **_Application DBAs_** do all the tasks of a general DBA focusing solely on a database for a specific application or set of applications, such as customer-service software. They may write or debug programs and must be able to manage the applications that work with the database. [<- Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-1)[Work Environment ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-3) ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 10: Database administrators](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1812.jpg) Database administrators are often referred to as DBAs. Database administrators held about 75,000 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of database administrators were as follows: Computer systems design and related services 14% Finance and insurance 12 Educational services; state, local, and private 11 Information 8 Management of companies and enterprises 7 Database architects"}
{"source_id": "CP-S009", "chunk_id": "CP-S009-0005", "chunk_index": 4, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Database Administrators and Architects", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["database", "cloud", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.490145+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "the applications that work with the database. [<- Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-1)[Work Environment ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-3) ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 10: Database administrators](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1812.jpg) Database administrators are often referred to as DBAs. Database administrators held about 75,000 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of database administrators were as follows: Computer systems design and related services 14% Finance and insurance 12 Educational services; state, local, and private 11 Information 8 Management of companies and enterprises 7 Database architects held about 69,500 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of database architects were as follows: Computer systems design and related services 23% Finance and insurance 15 Management of companies and enterprises 9 Administrative and support services 6 Computing infrastructure providers, data processing, web hosting, and related services 6 Database administrators and architects work in nearly all industries. For example, in retail they may design databases that track buyers’ shipping information; in healthcare, they may manage databases that secure patients’ medical records. ### Work Schedules Most database administrators and architects work full time. [<- What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-2)[How to Become One ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-4) ## How to Become a Database Administrator or Architect[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image 11: Database administrators](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/3640.jpg) Database administrators usually have a bachelor’s degree in an information- or computer-related subject such as computer science. Database administrators (DBAs) and architects typically need a bachelor’s degree in computer and information technology or a related field. ### Education Database administrators and architects typically need a bachelor’s degree in [computer and information technology](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/computer-and-information/computer-and-information-technology-field-of-degree.htm) or a related [field](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/home.htm), such as [engineering](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/engineering/engineering-field-of-degree.htm); some DBAs study [business](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/business/business-field-of-degree.htm). Employers may prefer to hire applicants who have a master’s degree focusing on data or database management, typically either in computer science, information systems, or information technology. Database administrators and architects need an understanding of database languages, such as Structured Query Language, or SQL.DBAs will need to become familiar with whichever programming language their firm uses. ### Licenses, Certifications, and Registrations Certification is typically offered directly from software vendors or vendor-neutral certification providers. Employers may require their database administrators and architects to be certified in the products they use. ### Advancement Database administrators and architects may advance to become [computer and information systems managers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/management/computer-and-information-systems-managers.htm \"Computer and Information Systems Managers\"). Experienced database administrators may advance to become database architects. ### Important Qualities The following are examples of qualities that are important for these workers to perform their duties. For more information, visit the Employment Projections (EP) [skills data page](https://www.bls.gov/emp/data/skills-data.htm). **_Analytical skills_.**DBAs monitor a database system’s performance to determine when action is needed. They must evaluate information from a variety of sources to decide on an approach. **_Communication skills_.** Most database administrators and architects work on teams and need to convey information effectively to developers, managers, and"}
{"source_id": "CP-S009", "chunk_id": "CP-S009-0006", "chunk_index": 5, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Database Administrators and Architects", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["database", "cloud", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.490145+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Qualities The following are examples of qualities that are important for these workers to perform their duties. For more information, visit the Employment Projections (EP) [skills data page](https://www.bls.gov/emp/data/skills-data.htm). **_Analytical skills_.**DBAs monitor a database system’s performance to determine when action is needed. They must evaluate information from a variety of sources to decide on an approach. **_Communication skills_.** Most database administrators and architects work on teams and need to convey information effectively to developers, managers, and other workers. **_Detail oriented_.** Working with databases requires an understanding of complex systems, in which a minor error can cause major problems. **_Problem-solving skills_.** When database problems arise, administrators and architects must troubleshoot and correct the problems. [<- Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-3)[Pay ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-5) ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Database Administrators and Architects Median annual wages, May 2025 Database architects $139,500 Database administrators and architects $126,760 Computer occupations $109,470 Database administrators $104,620 Total, all occupations $50,980 Note: Data are from the Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics program. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. The median annual wage for database administrators was $104,620 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $60,230, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $163,320. The median annual wage for database architects was $139,500 in May 2025. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $86,240, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $204,000. In May 2025, the median annual wages for database administrators in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Management of companies and enterprises$124,270 Computer systems design and related services 124,130 Finance and insurance 123,280 Information 121,800 Educational services; state, local, and private 86,330 In May 2025, the median annual wages for database architects in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Computing infrastructure providers, data processing, web hosting, and related services$152,560 Administrative and support services 149,490 Computer systems design and related services 148,680 Finance and insurance 146,470 Management of companies and enterprises 139,320 Most database administrators and architects work full time. [<- How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-4)[Job Outlook ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-6) ## Job Outlook[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-job-outlook) ### Database Administrators and Architects Percent change in employment, projected 2025–35 Database architects 9% Computer occupations 6% Database administrators and architects 4% Total, all occupations 3% Database administrators 0% Note: Percent change in employment is rounded to the nearest whole number. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Overall employment of database administrators and architects is projected to grow 4 percent from 2025 to"}
{"source_id": "CP-S009", "chunk_id": "CP-S009-0007", "chunk_index": 6, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Database Administrators and Architects", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["database", "cloud", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.490145+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Outlook[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-job-outlook) ### Database Administrators and Architects Percent change in employment, projected 2025–35 Database architects 9% Computer occupations 6% Database administrators and architects 4% Total, all occupations 3% Database administrators 0% Note: Percent change in employment is rounded to the nearest whole number. All Occupations includes all occupations in the U.S. Economy. Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Overall employment of database administrators and architects is projected to grow 4 percent from 2025 to 2035, about as fast as the average for all occupations. About 7,300 openings for database administrators and architects are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### Employment Projected employment of database administrators and architects varies by occupation. (See table.) Database administrators and architects will be needed to organize and present information to stakeholders in a user-friendly format. As organizations continue to improve their systems and adopt artificial intelligence (AI) to process their data, database architects will be critical to ensuring proper database design, transition, backup, and security. Employment in this occupation is projected to grow in response to the growing need to provide quality data infrastructure that can support high-tech innovation. Demand for database administrators may be limited as fewer of these workers are expected to be needed as many companies operate in the cloud, allowing fewer administrators to serve more companies at the same time. Moreover, some database administrators are expected to upskill to other roles, such as database architects or [software developers](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/software-developers.htm). Employment projections data for database administrators and architects, 2025–35 | Occupational Title | SOC Code | Employment, 2025 | Projected Employment, 2035 | Change, 2025–35 | Employment by Industry | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values are rounded to the nearest 100 or percent. Data is unavailable for values denoted with a \"—\". Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. | | **Database administrators and architects** | — | 144,500 | 150,900 | 4 | 6,500 | — | | **Database administrators** | 15-1242 | 75,000 | 74,900 | 0 | -100 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-1242&ioType=o) | | **Database architects** | 15-1243 | 69,500 | 76,000 | 9 | 6,500 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-1243&ioType=o) | [<- Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-5)[State & Area Data ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-7) ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ### **Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)** The [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics](https://www.bls.gov/oes/) (OEWS) program produces employment and wage estimates annually for over 800"}
{"source_id": "CP-S009", "chunk_id": "CP-S009-0008", "chunk_index": 7, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Database Administrators and Architects", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["database", "cloud", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.490145+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "6,500 | — | | **Database administrators** | 15-1242 | 75,000 | 74,900 | 0 | -100 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-1242&ioType=o) | | **Database architects** | 15-1243 | 69,500 | 76,000 | 9 | 6,500 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-1243&ioType=o) | [<- Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-5)[State & Area Data ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-7) ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ### **Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)** The [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics](https://www.bls.gov/oes/) (OEWS) program produces employment and wage estimates annually for over 800 occupations. These estimates are available for the nation as a whole, for individual states, and for metropolitan and nonmetropolitan areas. The link below goes to OEWS data maps for employment and wages by state and area. Use the dropdown boxes to select an occupation. * [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) Profiles](https://data.bls.gov/oesprofile/) ### **Projections Central** Occupational employment projections are developed for all states by Labor Market Information (LMI) or individual state Employment Projections offices. All state projections data are available at [https://projectionscentral.org](https://projectionscentral.org/). Information on this site allows projected employment growth for an occupation to be compared among states or to be compared within one state. In addition, states may produce projections for areas; there are links to each state’s websites where these data may be retrieved. ### **CareerOneStop** CareerOneStop includes hundreds of [occupational profiles](http://www.careerinfonet.org/Occupations/select_occupation.aspx?next=occ_rep&level=&optstatus=111111111&id=1&nodeid=2&soccode=&stfips=&jobfam=&menuMode) with data available by state and metro area. There are links in the left-hand side menu to compare occupational employment by state and occupational wages by local area or metro area. There is also a [salary info tool](https://www.careeronestop.org/Toolkit/Wages/find-salary.aspx?frd=true) to search for wages by zip code. [<- Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-6)[Similar Occupations ->](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#tab-8) ## Similar Occupations[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/computer-and-information-technology/database-administrators.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-similar-jobs) This table shows a list of occupations with job duties that are similar to those of database administrators and architects. | | Occupation | Job Duties | ENTRY-LEVEL EDUCATION [![Image 12: Help on Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_help.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=inlinehelp-education) | 2025 MEDIAN PAY [![Image 13: Help on Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_help.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=inlinehelp-pay) | | --- | --- | --- | -