{"source_id": "CP-S010", "chunk_id": "CP-S010-0001", "chunk_index": 0, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Operations Research Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["analytics", "operations", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.629889+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Title: Operations Research Analysts URL Source: https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm Markdown Content: * [Summary](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-1) * [What They Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-2) * [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-3) * [How to Become One](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-4) * [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-5) * [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-6) * [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-7) * [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-8) * [More Info](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-9) ## Summary Please enable javascript to play this video. [Video transcript available at https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IBWYsytaCbw.](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IBWYsytaCbw) | Quick Facts: Operations Research Analysts | | --- | | [2025 Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-wage) | $88,940 per year $42.76 per hour | | [Typical Entry-Level Education](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-education) | Bachelor's degree | | [Work Experience in a Related Occupation](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-experience) | None | | [On-the-job Training](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-training) | None | | [Number of Jobs, 2025](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-number-jobs) | 113,100 | | [Job Outlook, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-outlook) | 12% (Much faster than average) | | [Employment Change, 2025–35](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=qf-emp-change) | 13,500 | ### [What Operations Research Analysts Do](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-2) Operations research analysts use mathematics and logic to help solve complex issues. ### [Work Environment](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-3) Operations research analysts spend much of their time in office settings, although travel may be necessary to meet with clients. Most operations research analysts work full time. ### [How to Become an Operations Research Analyst](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-4) Operations research analysts typically need at least a bachelor’s degree to enter the occupation. Some employers require or prefer that applicants have a master’s degree. Analysts may need a degree in operations research or a related field, such as applied mathematics. ### [Pay](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-5) The median annual wage for operations research analysts was $88,940 in May 2025. ### [Job Outlook](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-6) Employment of operations research analysts is projected to grow 12 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 7,500 openings for operations research analysts are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### [State & Area Data](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-7) Explore resources for employment and wages by state and area for operations research analysts. ### [Similar Occupations](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-8) Compare the job duties, education, job growth, and pay of operations research analysts with similar occupations. ### [More Information, Including Links to O*NET](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#tab-9) Learn more about operations research analysts by visiting additional resources, including O*NET, a source on key characteristics of workers and occupations. ## What Operations Research Analysts Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 1: Operations research analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1832.jpg) Operations research analysts advise managers and other decision makers on the appropriate course of action to solve a problem. Operations research analysts use mathematics and logic to help organizations make informed decisions and solve problems. ### Duties Operations research analysts typically do the following: * Identify problems in areas such as business, logistics, healthcare, or other fields * Collect and organize information from a"}
{"source_id": "CP-S010", "chunk_id": "CP-S010-0002", "chunk_index": 1, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Operations Research Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["analytics", "operations", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.629889+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "Operations Research Analysts Do[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-what-they-do) ![Image 1: Operations research analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1832.jpg) Operations research analysts advise managers and other decision makers on the appropriate course of action to solve a problem. Operations research analysts use mathematics and logic to help organizations make informed decisions and solve problems. ### Duties Operations research analysts typically do the following: * Identify problems in areas such as business, logistics, healthcare, or other fields * Collect and organize information from a variety of sources, such as databases, sales histories, and customer feedback * Gather input from workers or subject-matter experts * Analyze collected data and extract information relevant to the problem being addressed * Develop and test quantitative models, support software, and analytical tools * Write memos, reports, and other documents explaining their findings and recommendations for managers, executives, and other officials Operations research analysts may be involved in many aspects of an organization. For example, they may help managers decide how to allocate resources, develop production schedules, oversee the supply chain, and set prices. To begin a project, analysts first identify the problem to be solved or the processes to be improved. They typically collect data and interview clients, workers, or others involved in the business processes being examined. Analysts then break down the problem into its various parts using statistical and database software and analytical techniques, such as forecasting and data mining. They also study the effect that different changes and circumstances would have on each of these parts. For example, to help an airline schedule flights and set ticket prices, analysts may take into account the cities involved, the amount and cost of fuel required, the expected number of passengers, the pilots’ schedules, and the maintenance costs. Operations research analysts provide alternatives to pursuing different actions and may assist in achieving a consensus on how to proceed. They weigh the costs and benefits of alternative solutions or approaches in their recommendations to managers. ## Work Environment[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-working-environment) ![Image 2: Operations research analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1834.jpg) Operations research analysts typically work in an office setting. Operations research analysts held about 113,100 jobs in 2025. Some of the largest employers of operations research analysts were as follows: Finance and insurance 26% Professional, scientific, and technical services 22 Management of companies and enterprises 9 Federal government 6 Manufacturing 4 Some operations research analysts in the federal government work for the Department of Defense, which also employs analysts through private consulting firms. Operations research analysts spend much of their time in office settings. They may travel to gather information, observe business processes, work with clients, or attend conferences. ### Work Schedules Most operations research analysts work full time. ## How to Become an Operations Research Analyst[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image"}
{"source_id": "CP-S010", "chunk_id": "CP-S010-0003", "chunk_index": 2, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Operations Research Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["analytics", "operations", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.629889+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "9 Federal government 6 Manufacturing 4 Some operations research analysts in the federal government work for the Department of Defense, which also employs analysts through private consulting firms. Operations research analysts spend much of their time in office settings. They may travel to gather information, observe business processes, work with clients, or attend conferences. ### Work Schedules Most operations research analysts work full time. ## How to Become an Operations Research Analyst[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-become-one) ![Image 3: Operations research analysts](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/3642.jpg) Operations research analysts typically need at least a bachelor’s degree to enter the occupation. Operations research analysts typically need at least a bachelor’s degree to enter the occupation. Some employers require or prefer that applicants have a master’s degree. Analysts may need a degree in operations research or a related field, such as applied mathematics. ### Education Operations research analysts typically need at least a bachelor’s degree, but some jobs require a master’s degree. Fields of [degree](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/home.htm) may include operations research or a related field, such as [business](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/business/business-field-of-degree.htm), [mathematics](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/mathematics/mathematics-field-of-degree.htm), [engineering](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/engineering/engineering-field-of-degree.htm), or [computer science](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/field-of-degree/computer-and-information/computer-and-information-technology-field-of-degree.htm). Because operations research is based on quantitative analysis, students need extensive coursework in mathematics. Coursework in computer science is important because analysts rely on statistical and database software to assess and model data. ### Other Experience Some operations research analysts are veterans of the U.S. Armed Forces. Certain positions may require applicants to undergo a background check in order to obtain a security clearance. ### Important Qualities The following are examples of qualities that are important for these workers to perform their duties. For more information, visit the Employment Projections (EP) [skills data page](https://www.bls.gov/emp/data/skills-data.htm). **_Analytical skills._** Operations research analysts use a range of methods, including forecasting and data mining, to examine and interpret data. **_Communication skills._** Operations research analysts write memos, reports, and other documents and often present their data and conclusions to managers and other executives. They must be able to convey technical information in a way that is understandable to nontechnical audiences. **_Critical-thinking skills._** Operations research analysts must be able to organize information and make connections between ideas and facts. **_Interpersonal skills._** Operations research analysts typically work on teams. They also need to be able to persuade managers and executives to accept their recommendations. **_Math skills._** The models and methods used by operations research analysts are rooted in statistics, calculus, linear algebra, and other mathematics disciplines. **_Problem-solving skills._** Operations research analysts need to be able to diagnose problems and study relevant information to solve them. ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Operations Research Analysts Median annual wages, May 2025 Mathematical science occupations $107,570 Operations research analysts $88,940 Total, all occupations $50,980 The median annual wage for operations research analysts was $88,940 in May 2025. The median wage"}
{"source_id": "CP-S010", "chunk_id": "CP-S010-0004", "chunk_index": 3, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Operations Research Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["analytics", "operations", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.629889+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "by operations research analysts are rooted in statistics, calculus, linear algebra, and other mathematics disciplines. **_Problem-solving skills._** Operations research analysts need to be able to diagnose problems and study relevant information to solve them. ## Pay[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-pay) ### Operations Research Analysts Median annual wages, May 2025 Mathematical science occupations $107,570 Operations research analysts $88,940 Total, all occupations $50,980 The median annual wage for operations research analysts was $88,940 in May 2025. The median wage is the wage at which half the workers in an occupation earned more than that amount and half earned less. The lowest 10 percent earned less than $57,060, and the highest 10 percent earned more than $159,910. In May 2025, the median annual wages for operations research analysts in some of the top industries in which they worked were as follows: Federal government$139,980 Manufacturing 108,120 Management of companies and enterprises 97,610 Professional, scientific, and technical services 94,990 Finance and insurance 80,870 Most operations research analysts work full time. ## Job Outlook[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-job-outlook) ### Operations Research Analysts Percent change in employment, projected 2025–35 Mathematical science occupations 25% Operations research analysts 12% Total, all occupations 3% Employment of operations research analysts is projected to grow 12 percent from 2025 to 2035, much faster than the average for all occupations. About 7,500 openings for operations research analysts are projected each year, on average, over the decade. Many of those openings are expected to result from the need to replace workers who transfer to different occupations or exit the labor force, such as to retire. ### Employment As technology advances and companies and government agencies seek efficiency and cost savings, demand for operations research analysis is expected to continue to grow. In addition, increasing demand will likely occur for these workers in the field of analytics to improve business planning and decision making. Technological advances have made it faster and easier for organizations to get data. Operations research analysts manage and evaluate data to improve business operations, supply chains, pricing models, and marketing. In addition, improvements in analytical software have made operations research more affordable and applicable to a wider range of areas. More companies are expected to employ operations research analysts to help them turn data into information that managers use to make decisions about all aspects of their business. Employment projections data for operations research analysts, 2025–35 | Occupational Title | SOC Code | Employment, 2025 | Projected Employment, 2035 | Change, 2025–35 | Employment by Industry | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values"}
{"source_id": "CP-S010", "chunk_id": "CP-S010-0005", "chunk_index": 4, "publisher": "BLS", "title": "Operations Research Analysts", "category": "occupations", "topics": ["analytics", "operations", "skills"], "url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "resolved_url": "https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm", "retrieval_method": "reader_fallback", "fetched_at": "2026-09-21T14:55:43.629889+00:00", "http_status": 403, "content_type": "text/plain", "word_count": 450, "text": "make decisions about all aspects of their business. Employment projections data for operations research analysts, 2025–35 | Occupational Title | SOC Code | Employment, 2025 | Projected Employment, 2035 | Change, 2025–35 | Employment by Industry | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Percent | Numeric | | Note: Employment subcategories do not necessarily add to higher categories due to rounding. Employment and projected employment change values are rounded to the nearest 100 or percent. Data is unavailable for values denoted with a \"—\". Source: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. | | **Operations research analysts** | 15-2031 | 113,100 | 126,600 | 12 | 13,500 | [Get data](https://data.bls.gov/projections/nationalMatrix?queryParams=15-2031&ioType=o) | ## State & Area Data[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-state-area) ### **Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)** The [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics](https://www.bls.gov/oes/) (OEWS) program produces employment and wage estimates annually for over 800 occupations. These estimates are available for the nation as a whole, for individual states, and for metropolitan and nonmetropolitan areas. The link below goes to OEWS data maps for employment and wages by state and area. Use the dropdown boxes to select an occupation. * [Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) Profiles](https://data.bls.gov/oesprofile/) ### **Projections Central** Occupational employment projections are developed for all states by Labor Market Information (LMI) or individual state Employment Projections offices. All state projections data are available at [https://projectionscentral.org](https://projectionscentral.org/). Information on this site allows projected employment growth for an occupation to be compared among states or to be compared within one state. In addition, states may produce projections for areas; there are links to each state’s websites where these data may be retrieved. ### **CareerOneStop** CareerOneStop includes hundreds of [occupational profiles](http://www.careerinfonet.org/Occupations/select_occupation.aspx?next=occ_rep&level=&optstatus=111111111&id=1&nodeid=2&soccode=&stfips=&jobfam=&menuMode) with data available by state and metro area. There are links in the left-hand side menu to compare occupational employment by state and occupational wages by local area or metro area. There is also a [salary info tool](https://www.careeronestop.org/Toolkit/Wages/find-salary.aspx?frd=true) to search for wages by zip code. ## Similar Occupations[About this section](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/operations-research-analysts.htm#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=about-similar-jobs) This table shows a list of occupations with job duties that are similar to those of operations research analysts. | | Occupation | Job Duties | ENTRY-LEVEL EDUCATION [![Image 4: Help on Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_help.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=inlinehelp-education) | 2025 MEDIAN PAY [![Image 5: Help on Median Pay](https://www.bls.gov/images/icons/icon_small_help.gif)](https://www.bls.gov/#TB_inline?height=325&width=325&inlineId=inlinehelp-pay) | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | [![Image 6: data-scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/96313.png)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm) | **[Data Scientists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/math/data-scientists.htm)** | Data scientists use analytical tools and techniques to extract meaningful insights from data. | Bachelor's degree | $120,230 | | [![Image 7: Economists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/images/1522.jpg)](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/economists.htm) | **[Economists](https://www.bls.gov/ooh/life-physical-and-social-science/economists.htm)** | Economists conduct research, prepare reports, and evaluate issues related to monetary and fiscal policy. They also may collect and analyze statistical data. | Master's degree | $124,720 |"}
