allation of computer programs and systems. Related occupations Use the computer in the analysis and solution of business problems, such as development of integrated production and inventory control and cost analysis systems. Related occupations Coordinate and link the computer systems within an organization to increase compatibility so that information can be shared. Related occupations Use object-oriented programming languages, as well as client and server applications development processes and multimedia and Internet technology. Related occupations Analyze information processing or computation needs and plan and design computer systems, using techniques such as structured analysis, data modeling, and information engineering. Related occupations Consult with management to ensure agreement on system principles. Related occupations Specify inputs accessed by the system and plan the distribution and use of the results. Related occupations Expand or modify system to serve new purposes or improve work flow. Related occupations Train staff and users to work with computer systems and programs. Related occupations Assess the usefulness of pre-developed application packages and adapt them to a user environment. Related occupations Determine computer software or hardware needed to set up or alter systems. Related occupations Read manuals, periodicals, and technical reports to learn how to develop programs that meet staff and user requirements. Related occupations Develop, document, and revise system design procedures, test procedures, and quality standards. Related occupations Recommend new equipment or software packages. Related occupations Define the goals of the system and devise flow charts"}
